////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// Acid Test: Cong Chains
//
// Transitive chains may mix = with congruence steps cong_(m) (the
// cong-chains upgrade, 2026-07): a chain commits to one operator family
// - the inequality family =/</≤ or the congruence family =/cong_(m) -
// AND, for congruences, to one modulus (the equal-moduli commitment:
// congruences compose only when the moduli agree, so a cong step with a
// structurally different modulus ends the chain at the parser).  The
// ChainsRule tool therefore always emits the per-family transitive
// conclusion; the only way a mixed-moduli trans_chain LC can exist is
// raw putdown that bypassed the parser, and the third section certifies
// that defensive path: such a chain still splits into steps, but emits
// no transitivity instantiation.
//
// The last section checks the by-algebra plumbing: the by-algebra tool
// converts a trio's two sides to Algebrite syntax structurally from the
// LC (algebraToCAS, 2026-07-28), so an = step justified by algebra
// works inside a cong chain - and works HERE, in a test file whose LCs
// carry no lurchNotation attribute at all, which the old
// notation-string slicing could never do.
//
// Each section below uses its own disjoint constants so that rules and
// instantiations cannot cross-justify between sections.

Declare =, cong, ⋅, +
Rule: :{ ChainsRule }

➤ "a mixed =/cong chain: steps validate, and the transitive conclusion is the congruence"
{
  Declare a, b, c, m
  {
    Assume a cong_(m) b
    Assume b = c
    { a cong_(m) b = c } ✔︎
    a cong_(m) c ✔︎
  }
}

➤ "a pure congruence chain: transitive conclusion, same modulus required"
{
  Declare x, y, z, k, j
  {
    Assume x cong_(k) y
    Assume y cong_(k) z
    { x cong_(k) y cong_(k) z } ✔︎
    x cong_(k) z ✔︎
    x cong_(j) z ✗
  }
}

➤ "defensive: a mixed-moduli trans_chain built in raw putdown still splits into steps but emits no transitive conclusion"
{
  Declare p, q, r
  {
    Assume p cong_(2) q
    Assume q cong_(3) r
    «{ (trans_chain p (≅ 2) q (≅ 3) r) }» ✔︎
    p cong_(2) r ✗
    p cong_(3) r ✗
  }
}

➤ "an = step by algebra inside a cong chain converts structurally (no notation attribute needed)"
Rule: :{ AlgebraRule }
{
  Declare u
  {
    Assume 2*u+2 cong_(2) 0
    { 2*(u+1) = 2*u+2 by algebra cong_(2) 0 } ✔︎
  }
}
